\details{
A population is a species in a site. The statistics of a species use its populations with at
least two individuals measured at the last survey, and need three such populations at sites
that differ in environment. \code{slope} and \code{r2} are the slope and \eqn{R^2}{R2} of a linear
regression of population means on the environment, \code{sd} the standard deviation of the means
and \code{resid_sd} that of the residuals. \code{within} is the logarithm of the mean variance within
populations (the phenotypic variance, genetic plus environmental) and \code{within_slope} the slope
of the variance within populations on the logarithm of abundance, positive when drift erodes
the genetic variance of small populations. \code{qst} is the residual variance among population
means, \eqn{V_B}{V_B}, relative to \eqn{V_B + 2 V_W}{V_B + 2 V_W}, where \eqn{V_W}{V_W} is
the mean variance within populations, a divergence among populations beyond what the
environment explains, in the form of \eqn{Q_{ST}}{QST} (Spitze 1993) but with phenotypes.
\code{near} and \code{far} are the semivariances of residuals between populations closer than \code{near}
and farther than \code{far}, relative to the residual variance, and \code{dist_cor} the correlation of
squared differences of residuals with distance: drift makes neighbours differ, gene flow
makes them alike. \code{size_cor} is the correlation of absolute residuals with the logarithm of
abundance, negative when small populations drift more.

With two or more surveys (\code{step}), the first and the last are compared, in the populations
with at least two individuals measured in both. \code{temporal} is the mean squared change of
population means minus its sampling variance, relative to the variance within populations,
and \code{temporal_slope} the slope of that corrected change on the inverse of abundance, the
basis of the temporal method of estimating the effective size (Waples 1989).

